  "title": "A force at a fixed angle to the path.",
  "body": " A force at a fixed angle to the path   An object moves along a circle of radius centred at and is acted upon by a force of constant magnitude . At every moment the force makes a angle with the unit tangent vector , as shown in . Calculate the work done by as the object moves along the half circle from to .   The object travels over the top of the circle from to . At every point the force (red) makes a angle with the unit tangent vector (green), the direction of travel.   A half circle traversed from A over the top to B, with a force arrow at thirty degrees to the tangent arrow at one point.   A circle of radius R centred at a point O. Its lower half is dashed; its upper half, the path C, is drawn thick with an arrowhead to the right of the top, so it is traversed clockwise from the point A at the left end of the horizontal diameter to the point B at the right end. A line segment from O to the upper right part of the circle is labelled R. At a point on the upper left of the path two arrows start: a green arrow labelled T, tangent to the circle and pointing up and to the right, and a longer red arrow labelled F that leans thirty degrees further up, away from the centre. A small arc between them is labelled thirty degrees.     h(t) = (-2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))  P = h(pi\/4)  tv = 1.25*(cos(pi\/4), sin(pi\/4))  fv = 1.9*(cos(5*pi\/12), sin(5*pi\/12))         C       O     R       A     B       \\mathbf{F}     \\mathbf{T}     30^\\circ                      Blank workspace for the work done by the force along the half circle.     "
